WHAT WE ADDED

ProClaim retrieves with PubMed and Semantic Scholar. The paper's "ProClaim
0.80" row retrieves with the librarian instead, and **that backend exists
nowhere upstream** — the word "librarian" does not appear in any branch of the
ProClaim repository. It is this repository's contribution.

  - `backend/librarian_search.py`, `backend/librarian_query_generator.py` —
    the backend itself: it runs an in-process `LibrarianAgent` and adds the
    papers and evidence snippets it returns to ProClaim's `EvidenceState`.
  - `backend/one_shot.py` — the single-pass verification path the librarian
    backend runs: one librarian search, fact extraction, a bounded
    sparse-evidence recovery loop, and a verdict. Also the librarian-adjusted
    subprocess environment, which adds the `AGENT_LLM_*` variables and drops
    the MLP classifier, since sufficiency is decided from extracted facts.
  - `backend/config.py` — `LibrarianSettings`, ProClaim's `VerificationSettings`
    plus `retrieval_backend` and the two librarian endpoint fields.
  - `backend/prompts.py`, `backend/model_registry.py` — the librarian system
    prompt, and the outer-agent model the verdict is emitted on.
  - `configs/*.yaml` — the three run configs.
  - `direct_entry.py` — the entry point that runs all of the above in place of
    `proclaim.verification.evidence_programming_direct`.

WHAT WE DID NOT MODIFY

**Nothing.** `ProClaim_src/` is a pristine clone at

    6316258ba31386657389fea5bb2db2c97362e06c

and `../setup.sh --check` asserts it is byte-for-byte that commit. Upstream's
own behaviour is not merely unchanged but unreachable from here: there is no
`retrieval_backend` switch left at a safe default inside ProClaim, because
ProClaim never learns the librarian exists.

That is possible because the integration is almost purely additive. Measured
against the version of this work that did patch the clone, it removed 13 lines
of upstream logic and added 1,852. The 13 were a dispatch at the top of
`verify_claim_direct()`; rather than change that dispatch, `direct_entry.py`
does not call it. The additions became `backend/`.

`backend/one_shot.py` borrows four helpers out of ProClaim's
`evidence_programming_direct` — `init_jupytext_log`, `append_to_jupytext_log`,
`generate_notebook` and `build_subprocess_env` — and otherwise stands beside it.
Those imports are the seam: if upstream renames one, the run fails at import,
loudly, rather than producing numbers from a pipeline that silently did
something else.

REPRODUCIBILITY

The "ProClaim 0.80" row was measured against the project's pre-release tree,
under its old package name `pkevolve`, before this public release existed. The
integration above is ported onto the release. The authors describe the release
as the cleaned-up version of that code, and nothing here depends on behaviour
the port changed — but the row has not yet been re-measured on it.

DATA

The two claim sets are ProClaim's own and are not redistributed here: they
arrive with the clone, at `ProClaim_src/datasets/{signor,connectomedb}.csv`,
and the harness reads them from there.
